---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import PageBody from "../components/PageBody.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "handle", label: "Handle" },
  { id: "layout", label: "Layout" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="bottom_sheet">
<PageBody sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          Use a <code>dialog.bottom-sheet</code> for additional or secondary
          content on compact and medium windows, not for the app's main view.
          The dialog is the surface. People dismiss it to get back to the page.
        </p>
        <p>
          Tokens follow the
          <a href="https://m3.material.io/components/bottom-sheets">M3 bottom sheet spec</a>.
          The container uses <code>surface-container-low</code>, with 28dp top
          corners and elevation 1. Max width is 640dp. From the Medium breakpoint
          the sheet is inset 56dp from the sides. It never covers the top
          72dp of the viewport. The drag handle is a 32×4dp bar in a 48dp
          hit target.
        </p>
        <p>
          The same sheet has two variants:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Modal</strong> uses <code>showModal()</code>. A scrim sits
            behind the sheet and the page is inert. Tap the scrim, drag the
            handle down, or submit a <code>&lt;form method="dialog"&gt;</code>
            to dismiss.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Standard</strong> uses <code>show()</code>. It has no
            scrim, and the page stays interactive. Drag the handle down or use a close
            action to dismiss.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          <button type="button" onclick="document.getElementById('sheet-modal').showModal()">Show modal</button>
          <button type="button" class="tonal" onclick="document.getElementById('sheet-standard').show()">Show standard</button>
        </p>

        <dialog id="sheet-modal" class="bottom-sheet" aria-labelledby="open-file-title">
          <h2 id="open-file-title">Open file</h2>
          <div>
            <ul class="list">
              <li>
                <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">description</span>
                Draft.md
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">image</span>
                Cover.png
              </li>
              <li>
                <span class="material-symbols" aria-hidden="true">folder</span>
                Archive
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <form method="dialog">
            <button type="submit" class="text" value="cancel">Cancel</button>
          </form>
        </dialog>

        <dialog id="sheet-standard" class="bottom-sheet" aria-labelledby="now-playing-title">
          <h2 id="now-playing-title">Now playing</h2>
          <p>Secondary controls for the current track. The page behind this sheet stays usable.</p>
          <form method="dialog">
            <button type="submit" class="text" value="close">Close</button>
          </form>
        </dialog>
<Code code={`
<button type="button" onclick="document.getElementById('sheet').showModal()">
  Show modal
</button>

<dialog id="sheet" class="bottom-sheet" aria-labelledby="open-file-title">
  <h2 id="open-file-title">Open file</h2>
  <div>…</div>
  <form method="dialog">
    <button type="submit" class="text" value="cancel">Cancel</button>
  </form>
</dialog>
`} />
        <p>
          Pick the variant by calling <code>showModal()</code> or
          <code>show()</code>. The modal variant has no extra class. A
          <code>::before</code> pseudo-element draws the handle. If you want a
          real element in that slot, put a
          <a href={route('drag_handle')}>drag handle</a> first.
        </p>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.handle}>
        <p>
          Drag the top 48dp (or an explicit
          <a href={route('drag_handle')}>drag handle</a>) down to
          dismiss the sheet. A recent flick counts even if the pointer stops
          moving at release. On a short drag, pausing for at least 100ms or
          reversing direction lets the sheet snap back. If the body overflows,
          the rest of the sheet scrolls.
        </p>
        <p>
          On touch screens, give the sheet an explicit drag handle. The handle
          sets <code>touch-action: none</code>. Without it, the browser takes a
          touch drag on the built-in band as a scroll.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<dialog class="bottom-sheet" aria-labelledby="title-title">
  <span class="drag-handle" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <h2 id="title-title">Title</h2>
  …
</dialog>
`} />
        <p>
          A <code>&lt;span&gt;</code> in that slot is decoration and does
          nothing on its own. Keyboard users do not need the bar. Dragging is a
          pointer gesture, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> already dismisses the sheet from
          the keyboard.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a visible dismiss control, make the handle a
          <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> and give it a name. Activating it with a
          pointer or with <kbd>Enter</kbd> closes the sheet, so dragging is not
          the only way to use it. A drag that snaps back does not also dismiss
          the sheet. The component tells the click that ends a drag apart from
          a tap, so the sheet stays open.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<dialog class="bottom-sheet" aria-labelledby="title-title">
  <button type="button" class="drag-handle" aria-label="Dismiss"></button>
  <h2 id="title-title">Title</h2>
  …
</dialog>
`} />
        <p>
          Add <code>expandable</code> to give the sheet two heights. It opens partly expanded, at <code>--md-comp-bottom-sheet-partial-height</code> (50% of the viewport). <code>.expanded</code> grows it to the full height, 72dp short of the top. The height moves on the sheet's spring.
        </p>
        <p>
          A drag up on the handle, or a flick up, expands the sheet. A drag down from expanded collapses it. A drag down from the partial height dismisses it as usual. Closing the sheet puts it back to partial.
        </p>
        <p>
          A handle <code>&lt;button&gt;</code> switches between the heights instead of closing. The component keeps its <code>aria-expanded</code> current, so name the button for the height, not for dismissal. Because the handle no longer closes the sheet, give an expandable sheet an explicit close control.
        </p>
<Code code={`
<dialog class="bottom-sheet expandable" aria-labelledby="filters-title">
  <button type="button" class="drag-handle" aria-label="Sheet height" aria-expanded="false"></button>
  <h2 id="filters-title">Filters</h2>
  <div>…</div>
  <form method="dialog">
    <button type="submit" class="text">Done</button>
  </form>
</dialog>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.layout}>
        <p>
          On compact windows (below 600px) the sheet is full-bleed and flush
          to the bottom. From
          the Medium breakpoint the sheet is at most 640dp wide with 56dp
          side inset. It always stops 72dp short of the top. On large
          screens a
          <a href={route('side_sheet')}>side sheet</a>
          or a basic dialog is usually a better fit. The bottom sheet still
          lays out if you open one there.
        </p>
        <p>
          The sheet slides in, and snaps back after a short drag, on the default spatial spring. <code>--md-comp-bottom-sheet-motion</code> and <code>--md-comp-bottom-sheet-motion-duration</code> read <code>--md-sys-motion-spring-default-spatial</code> and its <code>-duration</code> token, so <code>motion="expressive"</code> gives it a slight bounce. The sheet extends 24px below the viewport, so the bounce never uncovers the page under it. With reduced motion requested, it appears without moving.
        </p>
  </Section>
</PageBody>
</DocsLayout>
